<% survey = findings_persistence_survey %>
<% hold = Lab::TransitionRule::HOLD_SAMPLES %>
<% exit_samples = Findings::PersistenceSurvey::CONFIRMABLE_SAMPLES %>
<% threshold = Lab::TransitionRule::THRESHOLD %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Every other finding in this log counts transitions: DESIGN §1.2 says when a world enters
  the transitioned state, the engine's tracker reports the first epoch it does, and a sweep
  is scored on how many of its seeds got there. None of that says anything about what
  happens next. The
  <%= link_to "60 000-epoch re-run", finding_path("mutation-rate-long-horizon") %> already
  showed why that is a gap: one of its confirmed emergences ends with the world back in the
  soup it started in. So the question here is not how often emergence happens but whether it
  lasts — of the worlds that crossed, how many were still across at their last sample, how
  many climbed back out, and how long the ones that held held for.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Every terminal run in the lab with a transition epoch, whichever sweep queued it, read
  through the persistence summary derived from its own stored samples
  (<span class="mono">Runs::PersistenceSummaryService</span>). "In the transitioned state"
  is the engine's own rule applied sample by sample — <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>
  below <%= threshold %> with no alphabet collapse — so Rails re-reads a stored series by the
  criterion the engine used to flag it and never invents a second one. Leaving the state is
  that rule's hold in reverse: the state ends at the first of <%= exit_samples %> consecutive
  samples the rule rejects, mirroring the <%= hold %> further qualifying samples that confirm
  an entry, so a single sample flickering back over the threshold is no more a relapse than a
  single sample under it is a transition. A state that never ends persisted to the run's last
  sample. Beside it the summary records the largest <span class="mono">replicator_count</span>
  the run ever sampled and the epoch it first stood at.
</p>
<p>
  This is a survey and not a sweep. The runs below come from different grids at different
  budgets, chosen by nothing but having transitioned, so what follows is a count of outcomes
  and never a hazard.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<% if survey.any? %>
  <p>
    <strong>
      <% if survey.relapsed_count.zero? %>
        No transitioned world in the lab has left the state yet.
      <% elsif survey.relapse_share < 0.5 %>
        Emergence is not a terminal state, and it is not usually left either.
      <% else %>
        Emergence is not a terminal state, and more worlds leave it than hold it.
      <% end %>
    </strong>
    The lab has <%= pluralize survey.transitioned_count, "terminal run" %> across
    <%= pluralize survey.sweeps.size, "sweep" %> with a transition epoch, and a persistence
    summary behind <%= survey.summarised_count %> of them.
    <% if survey.summarised_count.positive? %>
      The split is <%= survey.persisted_count %> still in the transitioned state at their
      last sample against <%= survey.relapsed_count %> that climbed back out of it —
      <%= percent_value survey.relapse_share %> of the summarised runs relapsed within their
      own budget.
      <% if survey.epochs_persisted.any? %>
        The worlds held the state for
        <%= number_with_delimiter survey.shortest_persistence %> to
        <%= number_with_delimiter survey.longest_persistence %> epochs, a median of
        <%= number_with_delimiter survey.median_persistence %>.
      <% end %>
      <% if survey.counted_count.positive? %>
        <%= survey.counted_count %> of them counted a replicating cell at all, and those
        censuses peak at <%= count_value survey.census_peaks.first %> to
        <%= count_value survey.largest_census %> cells, a median of
        <%= count_value survey.median_census %>.
      <% else %>
        None of them counted a replicating cell at any sample, so the census says nothing
        about this set yet.
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <% if survey.summarised_count.positive? %>
    <p>
      <strong>The census splits the outcome in two.</strong> The transitioned state is the
      compression rule's, and a run can be in it without the census ever seeing a colony, so
      the two halves are worth separating. Of the
      <%= pluralize survey.counted_count, "summarised run" %> that counted a replicating
      cell, <%= survey.counted_persisted_count %> persisted and
      <%= survey.counted_relapsed_count %> relapsed. Of the
      <%= pluralize survey.uncounted_count, "run" %> whose census never left zero — or was
      never taken — <%= survey.uncounted_persisted_count %> persisted and
      <%= survey.uncounted_relapsed_count %> relapsed.
      <% if survey.uncounted_relapsed_count.positive? %>
        So part of the relapse count is worlds the census never saw as a colony
        at all, and reading the totals alone would bury that.
      <% end %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Persistence of every transitioned run">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Sweep</th>
          <th>Run</th>
          <th class="numeric">Transition epoch</th>
          <th class="numeric">Epochs persisted</th>
          <th class="numeric">Census peak (epoch)</th>
          <th>Outcome</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% survey.table_rows.each do |row| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to row.experiment.name, experiment_path(row.experiment) %></td>
            <td><%= link_to "seed #{row.run.seed}", run_path(row.run) %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter row.run.transition_epoch %></td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <%= row.epochs_persisted ? number_with_delimiter(row.epochs_persisted) : "—" %>
            </td>
            <td class="numeric mono">
              <% if row.counted? %>
                <%= count_value row.census_peak %>
                (<%= number_with_delimiter row.peak_epoch %>)
              <% elsif row.summarised? && row.persistence.sampled? %>
                0
              <% else %>
                —
              <% end %>
            </td>
            <td>
              <% if !row.summarised? %>
                <span class="text-muted">not summarised</span>
              <% elsif row.relapsed? %>
                <span class="badge badge-warning">relapsed</span>
              <% else %>
                <span class="badge badge-success">persisted</span>
              <% end %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    One row per terminal run the detector flagged, grouped by sweep. "Epochs persisted" is
    the span from the transition to the last sample the transition rule still accepted;
    "Census peak" is the largest <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> the run ever
    sampled, with the epoch it first reached it in brackets; "—" means the run never had a
    value.
    <% if survey.capped? %>
      The table stops at <%= Findings::PersistenceSurvey::MAX_ROWS %> rows; the counts above
      are over all <%= survey.transitioned_count %>.
    <% end %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No terminal run in this database has a transition epoch yet, so there is nothing to
    summarise: the counts above fill in as the lab finishes runs that crossed.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What this does not claim</h2>
<p>
  <strong>A persister persisted to its last sample, not forever.</strong> Every run here was
  stopped by its own budget rather than by the world settling anything, so "persisted" means
  "had not relapsed when the clock ran out". The longest span above is a lower bound on
  nothing but itself.
  <% if survey.unconfirmable_count.positive? %>
    Fewer than <%= exit_samples %> samples follow the crossing in
    <%= pluralize survey.unconfirmable_count, "persister" %>, which is not enough room for an
    exit to be confirmed in: they read as persisted because nothing could have flagged them
    otherwise.
  <% end %>
</p>
<p>
  <strong>No hazard is estimated.</strong> These runs are not an arm of anything — they are
  whatever transitioned across sweeps with different world sizes, mutation rates,
  interaction budgets and horizons — so the relapse share above is a description of this
  set of runs and not a rate the substrate has. Whether the hazard of relapse falls with a
  colony's age, which is the question the design record's first rung asks, cannot be read
  off a set assembled this way.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Entering and leaving are decided by compression alone.</strong> Both ends of the
  state are the <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> rule of §1.2 with its collapse
  guard; the census enters neither. A relapse in a run whose
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> never left zero is therefore a soup that briefly
  compressed and then stopped compressing, not a colony that died, and it is counted here
  because the transitioned state is defined that way — not because anything replicated.
  Every zero-census row above should be read as the detector's event ending, and only the
  rows with a census peak as a colony that was seen and then was not.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>The exit rule is a Rails-side definition.</strong> §1.2 and the engine own entry;
  leaving the state is defined over stored samples, at the sampling cadence each sweep chose.
  A world that dipped out and back between two samples reads as having held throughout, and
  a sparser cadence makes both the persistence span and the census peak coarser. By §2 the
  engine has to be the one that measures persistence, and it does not yet.
</p>
<p>
  This stays partial for both reasons at once: runs remain on the clock, so the denominators
  above move, and the set they are drawn from was assembled by accident rather than designed.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The <span class="mono">persistence</span> sweep of the evolution programme is what turns
  this survey into a measurement: the parameter points that already transition, at 20 seeds
  and a budget several times 60 000, so the relapse hazard has enough events and enough
  post-transition time to be fitted against colony age rather than merely counted. The
  observable belongs in the engine before then. Until it is run, the honest reading of the
  table is that a transitioned world is not safe: some of them come back.
</p>
